{"id":65,"date":"2014-11-03T02:39:25","date_gmt":"2014-11-03T02:39:25","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/agilewriter.wordpress.com\/?p=65"},"modified":"2014-11-03T02:39:25","modified_gmt":"2014-11-03T02:39:25","slug":"the-heros-journey-in-a-song","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/2014\/11\/03\/the-heros-journey-in-a-song\/","title":{"rendered":"The Hero&#8217;s Journey &#8211; In a Song"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><a href=\"https:\/\/agilewriter.files.wordpress.com\/2014\/11\/barrymanilow.jpg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-70 size-medium\" src=\"https:\/\/agilewriter.files.wordpress.com\/2014\/11\/barrymanilow.jpg?w=199\" alt=\"BarryManilow\" width=\"199\" height=\"300\" srcset=\"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2014\/11\/barrymanilow.jpg 1000w, https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2014\/11\/barrymanilow-199x300.jpg 199w, https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2014\/11\/barrymanilow-680x1024.jpg 680w, https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2014\/11\/barrymanilow-768x1157.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 199px) 100vw, 199px\" \/><\/a>At Agile Writers we use the Hero&#8217;s Journey and break it down into 8 stages to tell a compelling story. But compelling stories don&#8217;t come just from novels. Here is the Agile Storyboard\u00a0in a nutshell:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The Hero &#8211; learning about the hero in her ordinary world<\/li>\n<li>The Special World &#8211; something happens to the hero and she is cast into a special world and learns the rules of the special world<\/li>\n<li>Discovery &#8211; the hero is discovering who she is in the special world<\/li>\n<li>Growth &#8211; the hero masters the special world. She commits to her main goal, and things start to go badly for our hero<\/li>\n<li>Decline &#8211; things start to go badly for our hero<\/li>\n<li>Despair &#8211; getting the main goal looks impossible and someone close to the hero may die<\/li>\n<li>The Gathering Storm &#8211; the hero makes a new plan for getting the main goal and in the climax either gets the goal or not<\/li>\n<li>The Resolution &#8211; the subplots are resolved and we learn the message of the story.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Songwriters of years gone by and today still use this\u00a0classic structure to create compelling songs. Let&#8217;s look at the 1978 classic &#8220;Copacabana&#8221; by Barry Manilow. This song follows this structure, albeit abbreviated.<\/p>\n<p><b>&#8220;Copacabana (At The Copa)&#8221; Barry Manilow<\/b><\/p>\n<div>(<em>Establishing the Hero in her ordinary world)<\/em><\/div>\n<div>Her name was Lola, she was a showgirl<\/div>\n<div>With yellow feathers in her hair and a dress cut down to there<br \/>\nShe would merengue and do the cha-cha<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div><em>(The Special World and Introducing the Romantic Interest)<\/em><br \/>\nAnd while she tried to be a star<br \/>\nTony always tended bar<br \/>\nAcross the crowded floor, they worked from 8 til 4<br \/>\nThey were young and they had each other<br \/>\nWho could ask for more?<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div><i>[Chorus:]<\/i><br \/>\nAt the copa (CO!) Copacabana (Copacabana)<br \/>\nThe hottest spot north of Havana (here)<br \/>\nAt the copa (CO!) Copacabana<\/div>\n<div>Music and passion were always in fashion<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div><em>(Growth and the point of Commitment)<\/em><\/div>\n<div>At the copa&#8230;. they fell in love<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div><em>(IDecline and Introducing the Villain)<\/em><br \/>\nHis name was Rico<br \/>\nHe wore a diamond<br \/>\nHe was escorted to his chair, he saw Lola dancing there<br \/>\nAnd when she finished,he called her over<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div><em>(Despair,\u00a0Death, and The Climax)<\/em><br \/>\nBut Rico went a bit to far<br \/>\nTony sailed across the bar<br \/>\nAnd then the punches flew and chairs were smashed in two<br \/>\nThere was blood and a single gun shot<br \/>\nBut just who shot who?<i>[Chorus]<\/i><\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div>\n<p><em>(The Climax)<\/em><\/p>\n<div>At the copa&#8230; she lost her love<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div><em>(The Resolution)<\/em><\/div>\n<p>Her name is Lola, she was a showgirl,<br \/>\nBut that was 30 years ago, when they used to have a show<br \/>\nNow it&#8217;s a disco, but not for Lola,<br \/>\nStill in dress she used to wear,<br \/>\nFaded feathers in her hair<br \/>\nShe sits there so refined,and drinks herself half-blind<br \/>\nShe lost her youth and she lost her Tony<br \/>\nNow she&#8217;s lost her mind<\/p>\n<p><i>[Chorus]<\/i><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div><em>(The Delivery of the Message)<\/em><br \/>\nAt the copa&#8230; don&#8217;t fall in love<br \/>\nDon&#8217;t fall in love<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div>While not every element of the Hero&#8217;s Journey is not realized, the pattern is still apparent. Even in a 3-minute song the elements of great story telling is evident. If Barry Manilow can do it in 180 seconds, surely you can do it in 250 pages!<\/div>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>At Agile Writers we use the Hero&#8217;s Journey and break it down into 8 stages to tell a compelling story. But compelling stories don&#8217;t come just from novels. Here is the Agile Storyboard\u00a0in a nutshell: The Hero &#8211; learning about the hero in her ordinary world The Special World &#8211; something happens to the hero and she is cast into [&hellip;]<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":2,"featured_media":70,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"footnotes":""},"categories":[2,11],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-65","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-articles","category-heroes"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/65","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/2"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=65"}],"version-history":[{"count":0,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/65\/revisions"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/70"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=65"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/categories?post=65"},{"taxonomy":"post_tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/tags?post=65"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}